## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason about constraints in practical feature-flag settings and to design a solver using propagation and backtracking rather than pure search. It also exposes how to craft a constructive counterexample when needed.

## Key Concepts

- Constraint satisfaction with implications and disjunctions
- Unit propagation, backtracking, and a decision trail
- Real-world constraints in deployment feature flags and release gates

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal backtracker for boolean flags with given constraints
function sat(S){
  return (!S.S1 || S.S2) && (!S.S2 || S.S3) && (!S.S4 || !S.S1) && (S.S1 || S.S4) && (S.S2 || S.S4);
}
function satPartial(part){
  const v = (k)=> part.hasOwnProperty(k)? part[k] : null;
  if (v('S1')===true && v('S2')===false) return false;
  if (v('S2')===true && v('S3')===false) return false;
  if (v('S4')===true && v('S1')===true) return false;
  if (v('S1')===false && v('S4')===false) return false;
  if (v('S2')===false && v('S4')===false) return false;
  return true;
}
function solve(partial={}, idx=0){
  const vars=['S1','S2','S3','S4'];
  if (!satPartial(partial)) return null;
  if (idx===vars.length) return partial;
  const name = vars[idx];
  for (const val of [false,true]){
    const next = Object.assign({}, partial, {[name]: val});
    const res = solve(next, idx+1);
    if (res) return res;
  }
  return null;
}
console.log(solve());
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you scale this to thousands of flags with mostly Horn clauses?
- What optimizations would you add for large constraint sets?
